 ## Approach
 
-Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers display a revisioned view of the board. The approach for delivering committed changes is still open: five-second HTTPS polling, self-hosted WebSockets, or a hosted realtime service. All three can meet the required freshness target. Subsecond updates would be useful, but are not required.
+Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers display a revisioned view of the board. Committed changes are delivered via self-hosted WebSockets to achieve fast updates without tying the board to a realtime vendor. Browsers establish WebSocket connections to receive updates; the store maintains replayable revisions for reconnection recovery.
 
-Delivery, change storage, and browser recovery must be designed together after this choice. Polling would favor cursor queries; self-hosted sockets would need connection management and replay; hosted delivery would need publication reconciliation and provider credentials. Neither operating cost nor minimum operational burden has been declared the overriding criterion.
+The delivery layer manages connection lifecycle and session state; the change store maintains a revision sequence suitable for replay; browsers implement reconnect logic with revision-based catch-up. This accepts the operational burden of connection management and reconnect handling to avoid recurring realtime service fees and provider coupling.
 
